--- STDOUT ---
lx - File discovery, slicing, and formatting tool for LLM prompting.

Usage:
  lx [OPTIONS] [interleaved options] <actions>...
  lx [OPTIONS] -- <files...>

Description:
  lx is a specialized context bundler for Large Language Models (LLMs) like
  Claude, ChatGPT, and GitHub Copilot.

  It recursively walks directories, respecting .gitignore rules, detects binary
  files, and streams content into Markdown, XML/HTML, or bare text with
  token estimations.

  Stream Processing Model:
  lx processes arguments as a stream of sections. A section is a run of file
  and directory arguments that share the same processing settings. Interleaved
  options (like --lines or --include) are scoped to the section that follows
  them.

  Section boundaries are created in two ways:
    1. An interleaved option appears after files: all interleaved options
       reset to defaults before the new option takes effect.
    2. A -s (section) action is used after files: this also resets all
       interleaved options to defaults for the files that follow.

  Options that no file follows before a -s or the end of the arguments:
    - If files come right before them, they apply back to those files, with a
      warning, even when prompts or trees come between them and the -s.
    - Otherwise, as for options before the first file, they carry on into the
      section the -s starts. Options no file follows at all are reported, and
      so is a tree with no files to show.

  Example: last 50 lines of app.log, then src/ with line numbers:
    lx --tail 50 app.log -l src/
  The -l after app.log triggers a boundary; src/ gets a fresh default state
  with only line numbers enabled.

  Example: two named sections with different settings:
    lx -s "Backend" -l src/ -s "Frontend" -i "*.ts" web/
  Each -s resets interleaved options; web/ gets only the -i filter.

Arguments:
  [OPTIONS]
          Global options that affect the entire execution (e.g., --copy, --xml, --bare).
          These can be placed anywhere in the command.

  [interleaved options]
          Options that scope the processing rules for the next section.
          When placed after files, they reset all options before taking effect.
          Example: "-n 50 src/" limits src/ to 50 lines per file.

  [actions]
          The inputs to process. These can be:
            1. A file path (added directly).
            2. A directory path (walked recursively).
            3. An http/https URL (fetched and added as a file).
            4. An explicit action flag (-p, -s).
            5. "-" to read file paths from stdin.

          Archive URLs (.zip, .tar.gz, etc.) are expanded when -Z is active.

          As in rg, a path you name is never filtered itself: ignore files,
          hidden-file skipping and -i/-e do not apply to it. They filter what
          a named directory contains. Paths piped on stdin are filtered by
          -i/-e only, as a walk of the working directory would filter them,
          and a file piped twice, or with its directory, is listed once.

          If no actions are provided, lx walks the current directory ".".

  --
          Separator that disables flag parsing for subsequent arguments.
          Useful for filenames that start with a dash.

Global option:
  Settings that apply to the whole operation, regardless of position.
  Color is auto-detected; set NO_COLOR=1 to disable or FORCE_COLOR=1 to force.

  -c, --copy
          Copy the final formatted output directly to the system clipboard.
          
          This allows you to paste the context directly into an LLM interface
          without dealing with temporary files or pipe redirection.
          
          Supported system tools:
            - macOS: pbcopy
            - Linux: xclip (X11) or wl-copy (Wayland)
            - Windows: clip
  -o, --output <value>
          Write the formatted result to a specific file path.
          
          This is useful for generating large context bundles that you want to
          save locally.
  --md
          Format output as Markdown. This is the default output format.
          
          It wraps file contents in fenced code blocks (\```language) and
          separates files with horizontal rules. This format is widely
          understood by almost all coding assistants (ChatGPT, DeepSeek, GitHub
          Copilot).
  --xml
          Format output using XML tags.
          
          This format is highly recommended when working with Anthropic's Claude
          models. It wraps file content in <document> and <source> tags.
          <section> tags are especially recommended in xml mode.
  --html
          Format output as a standalone HTML 5 file.
          
          The output includes a responsive design (Pico CSS) and syntax
          highlighting (highlight.js). This is ideal for creating shareable
          static archives of code snippets, documentation, or logs that can be
          viewed in any browser.
  --bare
          Format output as plain text with minimal structure.
          
          This mode emits only file content and separates files with a single
          newline. No code fences, headers, section wrappers, or tree/prompt
          blocks are added.
  -C, --stdout
          Force output to stdout.
          
          This is the default behavior unless --copy or --output is specified.
          You can use this flag to override a "copy: true" setting in your
          configuration file when you specifically want to pipe the output to
          another tool.

  -0, --null
          Expect NUL-terminated filenames when reading from stdin.
          
          This is designed for pipeline compatibility with tools like 'find ...
          -print0' or 'fd ... -0', which use the null character to safely handle
          filenames containing spaces, newlines, or other special characters.
  --prompts-dir <value>
          Set the prompts library directory used by -P/--prompt-file.
          
          Overrides $LX_PROMPTS_DIR and the prompts_dir: config key. The default
          is ~/.config/lx/prompts when none of these are set.
  --list-prompts
          Print every prompt file in the configured prompts library and exit.
          
          Output is "<relative path>\t<absolute path>" per line, useful for
          shell completion or piping to a fuzzy finder:
          
            lx -P "$(lx --list-prompts | fzf | cut -f1)" src/
  -y, --config <value>
          Load configuration from a specific YAML file.
          
          Overrides default config paths (~/.config/lx/config.yaml or
          $LX_CONFIG). Configuration priorities are:
            1. Command line flags
            2. Specific config file (-y)
            3. Environment variable ($LX_CONFIG)
            4. User config directory
  -q, --quiet
          Suppress the final statistics summary and all non-error logging.
          
          Useful for clean piping where you don't want metadata on stderr
          interfering with scripts, though lx writes content to stdout and
          logs/stats to stderr by default anyway.
  -v, --verbose [value]
          Set the internal logging verbosity.
          
          Usage:
            -v, --verbose Sets level to Info
            -vv Sets level to Debug
            --verbose=debug Sets level to Debug
            --verbose=info Sets level to Info
            --verbose=warn Sets level to Warn
            --verbose=error Sets level to Error
            --verbose=silent Disables all logging
  --stats
          Force the display of the file count, size, and token summary to
          stderr.
          
          By default, statistics are hidden if output is being piped to another
          process (unless writing to a file or clipboard). This flag forces them
          to appear.
  --no-stats
          Disable the file count, size, and token summary footer entirely.
          
          Use this if you want the process to finish silently without printing
          metadata to the terminal.
  -V, --version
          Print the current version information and exit.
  -h, --help
          Show the help message. Use --help for the detailed manual style help.

Interleaved option:
  Options that configure the section which follows them. When placed before any
  files, they accumulate into the first section's settings. When placed after
  files, they trigger a section boundary: all interleaved options reset to
  defaults, then the new option applies to the next section. Options after
  files that no file follows apply back to those files.

  -l, --line-numbers
          Enable line numbers for the next section.
          
          This adds a numbered prefix (e.g., " 1: ") to every line of code.
          Extremely useful when asking an LLM to reference exact locations in
          follow-up prompts.
  -n, --lines <n>
          Limit the content of subsequent files to N lines.
          
          If a file is larger than N lines, lx will output the first N/2 lines
          and the last N/2 lines, separated by a visual gap indicator.
          
          Use 0 to enable compact mode: file content is skipped and only the
          filename and size are shown.
  --head <n>
          Read only the first N lines of subsequent files.
          
          Useful for inspecting headers, imports, or configuration files where
          the beginning is the most relevant part.
  --tail <n>
          Read only the last N lines of subsequent files.
          
          Useful for log files, append-only journals, or checking the most
          recent additions to a file.
  -i, --include <glob>
          Keep what matches a glob for subsequent paths, as rg -g GLOB does.
          
          -i and -e globs apply in command-line order, and the last one to match
          a path decides it: -e "build/*" -i build/keep.js keeps build/keep.js,
          while -i build/keep.js -e "build/*" drops it. Once there is an -i, a
          file must be kept by some glob. A path an -i keeps is kept even when
          an ignore file ignores it or it is hidden. A leading "!" makes -i
          exclude, as -e does.
          
          Globs follow .gitignore syntax: one without a slash matches a file or
          directory name anywhere, and one with a slash the whole path, which is
          the path from the working directory as you named the root: -i
          "src/*.go" src matches, -i "*.go" ../lib matches below "..", and -i
          "lib/*.go" ../lib does not. Below an absolute root the working
          directory is stripped, and outside it the absolute path is matched. A
          glob matches a directory only as itself, so -i src keeps nothing; use
          -i "src/**". An excluded directory is not entered, so no later glob
          keeps anything below it: -e build -i build/keep.js keeps nothing, -e
          "build/**" -i build/keep.js keeps it.
          
          As in rg, globs and the roots you name are matched as written, without
          cleaning: neither "./src/*.go" nor "src//*.go" matches src/main.go,
          and the files below the root src/../build are matched as
          "src/../build/...", though lx displays them as build/.... Paths piped
          on stdin are cleaned first. An -i that is empty or starts with "#"
          adds no glob, and a glob that cannot be parsed, such as "src/[ab", is
          an error.
          
          Examples:
            -i "*.go" (Keep only Go files)
            -i "src/**" (Keep the files inside src)
            -i "*.{js,ts}" (Keep JS and TS files)
  -e, --exclude <glob>
          Drop what matches a glob for subsequent paths, as rg -g '!GLOB' does.
          
          -e globs match as -i globs do, and the last -i or -e to match a path
          decides it, ahead of ignore files. As rg reads -g '!GLOB', two things
          differ from -i: an empty -e matches every path, so -e "" drops
          everything no later glob keeps, and a leading "#" is part of the glob,
          so -e "#x" drops a file named #x.
          
          A leading "!" keeps what matches, even when an ignore file ignores it
          or it is hidden, without requiring every other file to match some glob
          as -i does: -e "vendor/**" -e "!vendor/keep.go" keeps one file of
          vendor/ and everything outside it. rg has no such glob: its -g
          "!!glob" excludes a file named "!glob".
          
          Examples:
            -e "*_test.go" (Skip Go test files)
            -e "vendor/" (Skip vendor directories)
  -m, --max-size <size>
          Skip files larger than the given size for subsequent paths.
          
          Oversized files are dropped during discovery, before any content is
          read or converted, so a stray multi-gigabyte log or disk image cannot
          inflate the output. Skipped files are also omitted from the tree
          (-T/-t), so the tree keeps matching the actual output.
          
          The value is a byte count with an optional unit suffix. Suffixes are
          decimal and case-insensitive, matching how lx reports sizes elsewhere:
            -m 4096 (4096 bytes)
            -m 512k (512 kB)
            -m 2MB (2 MB)
            -m 1G (1 GB)
          
          The value must be greater than zero; there is no "unlimited" spelling,
          simply omit the flag.
          
          The limit applies to every input, including paths named directly.
          Inputs whose size is not known ahead of time, such as remote URLs, are
          never skipped.
  -D, --documents
          Convert document files to text for the next section.
          
          By default, document files are treated as binary. When set, the
          following formats are converted instead:
            - .pdf - via PDF text extraction
            - .docx - via Word document parsing
            - .xlsx - via spreadsheet cell values (one sheet per section)
            - .pptx - via presentation text extraction
            - .html, .htm, .xhtml - to structural Markdown
          
          HTML is the one format whose structure survives, because it is present
          in the input: headings become #, horizontal rules become ---, and
          lists, blockquotes, tables, links and fenced code blocks are
          preserved, with the code language taken from a highlight.js or prism
          class when present. Scripts, styles, navigation, headers, footers and
          asides are dropped, as are data: URIs, which is where the base64
          payloads that bloat a bundle live.
          
          Inline emphasis is deliberately not converted: it carries little
          signal for a model, and keeping it would mean escaping every literal *
          and _ in body text.
          
          The other formats yield plain text, which is as much as their
          extraction libraries expose.
  -M, --media
          Replace media files with a description of their metadata.
          
          By default, media files are binary and contribute nothing but a
          placeholder. When set, the header of the following formats is read
          instead:
            - video - .mp4 .m4v .mov .mkv .webm
            - audio - .m4a .mp3 .wav .flac
            - images - .png .jpg .jpeg .gif .webp .bmp .tiff .ico .avif .heic
          
          Reported are the duration, codecs, resolution and frame rate of a
          recording, or the dimensions, colour model and frame count of an
          image. Values are those the container states, which is not always what
          a decoder would report; an estimated duration is marked as one.
          
          The format is identified from the header, so a file with no suffix,
          such as most URLs, is recognised anyway, and one carrying the wrong
          suffix is read as what it actually is.
          
          Nothing is transcribed or captioned: the point is that the file's
          existence and shape reach the bundle, not its content. A file whose
          header cannot be parsed still reports its container. In HTML output,
          images are embedded whole instead.
  -Z, --expand
          Expand archive files inline for subsequent file arguments.
          
          When set, archive files are opened and their contents processed as if
          they were regular files in the directory tree. The archive path is
          used as a prefix for entries inside it (e.g. archive.zip/hello.txt).
          
          Include (-i) and exclude (-e) globs match an archive being expanded as
          a directory and its entries as the paths below it, whether it is found
          in a walk or named: -i "pkg/a.zip/src/**" keeps the entries of
          pkg/a.zip below src/. The entries of an archive URL are below the URL.
          Hidden entry filtering (leading dot) is also respected.
          
          Supported formats:
            ZIP-based:
              .zip, .jar, .war, .ear, .odt
            TAR (plain and compressed):
              .tar, .tar.gz, .tar.bz2, .tar.xz, .tar.zst,
              .tar.br, .tar.lz4, .tar.sz, .tar.s2,
              .tgz, .tbz2, .txz
            Other multi-file archives:
              .rar, .7z
            Single-file compression (exposed as one virtual entry):
              .gz, .bz2, .xz, .zst, .br, .lz4, .sz, .s2, .lz
  -H, --hidden
          Include hidden files and directories (starting with '.') for
          subsequent paths.
          
          By default, dotfiles and dot-directories are excluded from directory
          walks. This flag overrides that behaviour for the next section.
  -S, --follow
          Follow symbolic links that point to directories when walking.
          
          By default, directory symlinks are skipped to avoid cycles. This flag
          enables traversal into symlinked directories for the next section.
  --no-links
          Skip symbolic links to files when walking directories.
          
          By default, symlinks pointing to files are followed and their target
          content included. This flag suppresses them for the next section.
  -I, --no-ignore
          Disable ignore-file filtering for subsequent paths.
          
          By default, lx respects .ignore and .lxignore files and the global lx
          ignore file everywhere, and inside a git repository also .gitignore
          files, .git/info/exclude, and the global git ignore file. The ignore
          files of the directories above a path apply to it too. This flag
          disables that filtering so ignored files are included.
  -u, --functions
          Filter files in the next section to show only function and method
          signatures. A method keeps the header and closing line of the type
          around it for context; fields and types without methods are left out,
          and so are function pointers, which are variables. The directive rows
          of a C/C++ preprocessor conditional (#if ... #elif ... #else ...
          #endif) around kept lines are kept, except a header's include guard.
          
          Uses an AST-based parser to extract function definitions from source
          files. A "..." line marks where source lines were left out. Head/tail
          slicing counts the kept lines; line numbers (-l) and the skipped-rows
          gap count source rows.
          
          Pairs with --types (-Y) to show a full code skeleton.
  -Y, --types
          Filter files in the next section to show only type and class
          definitions, including their field/variable declarations. Methods are
          left out. Type aliases (type X = ..., typedef, using X = ...) are
          types, kept whole. The directive rows of a C/C++ preprocessor
          conditional (#if ... #endif) around kept lines are kept, except a
          header's include guard.
          
          Uses an AST-based parser to extract type definitions from source
          files. A "..." line marks where source lines were left out. Head/tail
          slicing counts the kept lines; line numbers (-l) and the skipped-rows
          gap count source rows.
          
          Pairs with --functions (-u) to show a full code skeleton.

Action:
  Explicit content generators that insert data into the stream immediately.

  -T, --tree
          Print a file tree for all files in the current section, then also
          include the file contents in the output. Use -t to suppress file
          content.
          
          The tree uses the same walker settings (filters, ignore rules) as the
          file listing, so it always matches the actual output.
          
          The flag can appear before or after the files it applies to:
            lx -T src (tree before src content)
            lx src -T (tree after src content)
            lx . -T (tree for the whole directory)
          
          A "section" is a set of consecutive file arguments with no interleaved
          options or section markers between them. Each section renders its own
          tree:
            lx -T src -s "Tests" test (tree only for src, not test)
            lx -T src -i "*.go" lib (two sections: tree for src, no tree for
            lib)
            lx -T src extra -- file2 (one section: tree covers src, extra,
            file2)
  -t, --tree-only
          Print a file tree for all files in the current section without
          including the file contents. This is useful for getting a structural
          overview.
          
          Behaves identically to -T except file contents are suppressed:
            lx -t src (show tree of src, no file content)
            lx -t . (show tree of current directory)
          
          See --tree (-T) for full details on section semantics.
  -s, --section <value>
          Insert a logical section separator with a custom header.
          
          This helps organize the output for the LLM by grouping related files.
          In Markdown mode this renders as a "## Header" block; in XML mode it
          wraps succeeding content in a named element.
          
          -s also acts as a section boundary for interleaved options (like -l,
          -n, -i, -e): after files, the section it starts begins with them all
          at their defaults. Options given since the last file decide where they
          go: if files came right before them, they apply back to those files,
          with a warning, whatever prompts or trees follow; otherwise, as for
          options before the first file, they carry on into the section -s
          starts.
          
          Example:
            lx -s "Backend" -l src/ -s "Frontend" -i "*.ts" web/
          The second -s resets -l so web/ gets full content, with only -i
          active.
  -E, --system-context
          Insert a block describing the machine and the command that produced
          the output.
          
          The block is placed where the flag appears, like -p and -T, so it can
          open the bundle or close it. It records the OS and architecture, the
          current time in ISO 8601, the hostname, the lx version, and the
          command line.
          
          This is for reproducibility: when you revisit a saved bundle weeks
          later, or collect context on several machines, the block says which
          environment the paths and tooling belong to. Platform-specific paths
          such as /nix/store/... are otherwise ambiguous.
          
          Example:
            lx --system-context -t ./project -o context.md
          
          Every field is also available individually to a custom meta_template
          as {{ .Fields.os }}, {{ .Fields.time }}, and so on. See CONFIG.md.
  -p, --prompt <value>
          Inject a custom text block directly into the output stream.
          
          Useful for short, inline instructions. To load a prompt from a file or
          from your prompts library, use -P/--prompt-file instead.
          
          Example:
            lx -p "Refactor the following code for better concurrency:" main.go
  -P, --prompt-file <value>
          Load a prompt from a file and inject it into the output stream.
          
          Value resolution (first match wins):
            - path-like input (absolute, ~, ./, ../) is read directly from disk
            - <prompts-dir>/<value> if it exists
            - <prompts-dir>/<value>.md|.txt|.prompt (override via
            prompt_extensions:)
            - recursive search by relative path or basename (no extension)
          
          Ambiguous basename matches error with the candidate paths so you can
          disambiguate using a longer relative path (e.g. "go/test").
          
          Library directory (first match wins):
            - --prompts-dir <path>
            - $LX_PROMPTS_DIR
            - prompts_dir: in the loaded config
            - ~/.config/lx/prompts
          
          Repeat -P to stack multiple prompts. See --list-prompts to enumerate
          the configured library.
          
          Examples:
            lx -P go/test src/foo.go -c
            lx -P refactor -i "*.py" src/ --xml -c
            lx -P plan -P comments docs/ src/

Examples:
  1. Code Review Bundle
     Copy all modified files in your git repo to the clipboard:
       $ git diff --name-only | lx -c

  2. Targeted Debugging
     Grab the last 50 lines of a log file, then the processing code with line
     numbers enabled for reference:
       $ lx --tail 50 /var/log/app.log \
            -l src/processor/

  3. Complex Filtering
     Find JavaScript files except tests, output in XML (optimized for Claude):
       $ lx --xml -i "*.js" -e "*test*" .

  4. Prompt Injection
     Load a prompt by name from your prompts library (set via $LX_PROMPTS_DIR
     or --prompts-dir), then attach the source code:
       $ lx -P go/test src/ -c
     Use -P with an explicit path for one-off prompt files:
       $ lx -P ./prompt.txt -s "Context" src/ -c
     Use -p only for inline literals:
       $ lx -p "Refactor for concurrency:" main.go

  5. Search Integration
     Use 'grep' to find files with TODOs and pipe them to lx:
       $ grep -Rl TODO | lx -c

  6. URL Input
     Fetch a remote file directly (no download step needed):
       $ lx https://example.com/config.yaml src/

     Expand a GitHub archive and filter to Go files only:
       $ lx -Z -i "*.go" https://github.com/owner/repo/archive/refs/heads/main.zip

Bugs can be reported on GitHub: https://github.com/rasros/lx/issues

--- STDERR ---
